1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,432
Storm diversion. One free slot left on
berth B7. Two reservation requests hit the
API within milliseconds.

2
00:00:09,551 --> 00:00:20,327
Optimistic locking would let both read
"one slot left," then fail one at commit —
acceptable for clerks who can retry,
painful when an automated gate must answer
now and only one ship may win.

3
00:00:20,448 --> 00:00:32,280
Pessimistic locking serializes access: the
first transaction locks the row; the
second waits or fails fast. JPA expresses
this with lock modes on find / query APIs.

4
00:00:32,399 --> 00:00:47,495
LockModeType.PESSIMISTIC_WRITE typically
becomes SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on databases
that support it. Look at this java for a
moment. Look at this java for a moment.
Walk the timeline.

5
00:00:47,615 --> 00:01:01,247
Transaction A calls findForUpdate("B7")
and holds the row lock. Transaction B’s
findForUpdate("B7") blocks until A commits
or rolls back — or times out, depending on
database and Spring lock timeout settings.

6
00:01:01,367 --> 00:01:14,087
After A commits with zero remaining slots,
B loads the fresh state and reserve(1)
throws a business exception instead of
overbooking. No version collision theater;
the database queue did the serialization.

7
00:01:14,207 --> 00:01:25,775
PESSIMISTIC_READ is a shared lock where
the dialect supports it — useful for read-
stable sections, less common for
reservation. PESSIMISTIC_WRITE is the
reservation tool.

8
00:01:25,896 --> 00:01:37,152
Lock timeout and deadlock detection are
operational concerns: set timeouts so a
stuck transaction does not freeze the
whole pier board, and keep the critical
section short — lock, decide, write,
commit.

9
00:01:37,272 --> 00:01:53,207
Do not lock, then call a remote tide API,
then write. Pessimistic locking is not
free. It reduces concurrency on hot rows.
Holding locks across user think-time is
how you recreate the worst of pessimistic
UIs.

10
00:01:53,328 --> 00:02:01,487
Prefer optimistic locking for ordinary
collaborative edits; reach for
PESSIMISTIC_WRITE when the business rule
is "exactly one winner,

11
00:02:01,608 --> 00:02:12,743
no retry loop." Using @Version and
PESSIMISTIC_WRITE on every entity "for
safety" fights itself — you pay lock costs
and still write version columns without a
clear policy.

12
00:02:12,864 --> 00:02:19,752
Locking in a non-transactional method also
fails: without a transaction boundary, the
lock has nowhere to live.

13
00:02:19,872 --> 00:02:31,848
And forgetting that FOR UPDATE behavior
varies by dialect leads to surprises when
tests on H2 differ from Postgres in
production. You can now choose optimistic
vs pessimistic for berth fights.

14
00:02:31,968 --> 00:02:45,048
Separately, the schedule board can still
feel slow even when no one is colliding —
not because of locks, but because of how
you load graphs, batch lazy collections,
and project columns. That is JPA
performance tuning.
